<.guide_page
  title={@guide_meta.title}
  dek={@guide_meta.dek}
  date={@guide_meta.date}
  read_time={@guide_meta.read_time}
>
  <p>
    AI agents are at their best when you stop babysitting them. Anyone who has used one for real
    work knows the shape: approve every command one at a time and you've bought a very slow
    autocomplete. Let it run and it reads the logs, chases a hypothesis, tries three things, hits a
    dead end, tries a fourth, and comes back with an answer while you were in a meeting.
  </p>
  <p>
    That freedom also lets mistakes reach further. An agent with production access can delete a
    database, wipe a volume, or "clean up" the wrong directory.
  </p>
  <p>
    For coding, a sandbox limits what the agent can reach. Tools like
    <a href="https://coop.dryga.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">co:op</a>
    <i>(our founder built it, so we're biased)</i>
    run the agent inside a container with limited access to files and services.
    It can still change the project you share and send files it can read to its model provider.
    Keep secrets out of reach and review the changes before using them.
  </p>
  <p>
    A sandbox around the agent won't undo a <code>DROP TABLE</code>
    it runs against your production database. To work on live infrastructure, the agent needs
    limits on which actions it can run there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="copy-paste">You start by copy-pasting</h2>
  <p>
    The first version of "AI for ops" at most teams involves no access at all: you paste a log or a
    screenshot into the chat, the model suggests a command, you copy it into a terminal, run it,
    and paste the result back. The model never touches your servers this way, and
    nothing runs unless you run it. For one-off troubleshooting, it's fine.
  </p>
  <p>
    But you are the whole loop. You carry the context one way and the commands the other, and the
    model only knows what you remembered to paste. You are also the only check, and that check
    decays: the first unfamiliar command gets read carefully, the tenth gets skimmed, and
    eventually "review" is just paste-and-Enter on a script you didn't really read. By then,
    generated code reaches your shell through a reflex. The
    <.link navigate={~p"/compare/copy-paste-ai-ops"}>copy-paste AI ops comparison</.link>
    looks at when this approach is enough and when it gets in the way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="raw-ssh">Give the AI agent direct SSH access</h2>
  <p>
    The obvious upgrade is a shell. And when agents run without a sandbox, sometimes you grant it
    without knowing: the agent finds your <code>gcloud</code>
    already authenticated, or finds your SSH keys, and connects to production. The agent already
    knows how to use SSH, so there's little setup. The risk is how much that connection lets it do.
  </p>
  <p>
    With unrestricted production access, the model can do anything your account can do.
    It decides whether to tail the logs or delete the data directory. A
    <.link navigate={~p"/guides/prompt-injection-for-ops-teams"}>prompt-injected</.link>
    log line can steer that decision. Without separate session recording, the shell history may be
    your only record of the commands. See the <.link navigate={~p"/compare/raw-ssh-for-ai"}>comparison with unrestricted SSH access</.link>.
  </p>
  <p>
    SSH access brokers can limit commands, require approval, record sessions and issue short-lived
    credentials. Keeping the private key away from the agent prevents it from copying the key.
    You still need to limit what it can do through the connection, including command arguments
    and whether it can start another shell.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp-per-tool">An MCP server per tool (better, until you count them)</h2>
  <p>
    The better instinct is <a
      href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >MCP</a>: give the agent tools with defined inputs instead of a shell. There's an MCP server for Postgres, one for Kubernetes,
    and one for your cloud. Choose tools that limit which actions and arguments the agent can
    request. An MCP server that exposes an unrestricted shell gives you the same problem through
    a different interface.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each server brings its own setup, credentials and upgrades. Access controls and audit logs
    vary too. You have to bring them together to answer two questions: what can the agent do
    right now, and what did it do last Tuesday? MCP defines how tools are called, but each server
    decides how to control access.
  </p>
  <p>
    Coverage matters too. A server for your database may be easy to find, while a safe tool for
    your RAID controller or the vendor appliance in rack 4 takes work. You need to review what
    each tool can run and which credentials it holds. Leaving unrestricted SSH available "just
    for the rest" gives the agent a way around the limits you set elsewhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-mcp">Where emisar fits</h2>
  <p>
    emisar gives the agent one catalog of actions. Each action defines a command and the
    arguments it accepts. Actions can read service logs, check replication lag or restart
    a unit. The <.link navigate={~p"/packs"}>pack catalog</.link>
    covers databases, queues, proxies and containers, along with host tasks such as checking
    ZFS pools, WireGuard tunnels and clock sync. You can add your own host commands as actions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your <.link navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}>access-control rules</.link>
    decide which actions run, which wait for approval and which are denied. One audit log keeps
    every request with its inputs, the decision and who asked, including denied requests.
    For actions that ran, it also keeps the redacted output.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent can use the same actions through the CLI or MCP.
    Use the <.link navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference#command-line"}>CLI</.link>
    from a terminal or script, or connect an AI client that supports MCP tools. A runner on each
    host connects out to the control plane, checks every argument again and applies your
    redaction rules before returning the output. The
    <.link navigate={~p"/security"}>security model</.link>
    explains why the host, not the cloud, gets the last word.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the
    <.link navigate={~p"/docs/investigate-a-service"}>service investigation walkthrough</.link>
    to try an investigation and see the command output. You can also compare <.link navigate={
      ~p"/compare/raw-ssh-for-ai"
    }>emisar with unrestricted SSH access</.link>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="read-production-write-code">Read production, write code</h2>
  <p>
    If you manage infrastructure as code, the agent can investigate production and prepare the
    fix in a pull request. We use Terraform a lot, and this is our preferred workflow.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let the agent run the diagnostic actions it needs without waiting for you. Choose those actions
    for the information they expose: logs and query results can contain credentials or customer data.
    Reading is most of the job: tail the logs, check the failing unit, compare replication lag across
    replicas, watch the error rate move. The agent keeps its eyes on real production while it
    investigates, so it doesn't work from stale dashboards or ask you to paste the output.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent writes the Terraform diff or the Ansible
    change back in its own sandbox and opens a pull request. The fix then ships through review and your
    pipeline like any other change. Production stays declarative and reproducible, so you don't wake
    up to a prod that an agent hand-patched into a snowflake nobody can rebuild.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some incidents need an immediate change: restart a stuck service, fail over or stop a runaway
    query. Set those actions to require approval. You review the command and its target, then
    approve it without having to type it yourself. Use staging to test risky changes before
    they reach production.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start-small">Start small</h2>
  <p>
    Run coding agents in a sandbox that keeps your home directory and production credentials
    out of reach.
  </p>
  <p>
    Give the agent read-only actions on a non-critical production service and watch it investigate.
    Add actions as it needs them, with approval for changes you want to review. Each action has
    an access rule and a record of its use. The
    <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}>quickstart</.link>
    takes about five minutes.
  </p>
</.guide_page>
